                <h2>Conclusion First: Running Doesn't Mean Selling</h2>
<p>In Meta advertising, "high-performing" creatives don't necessarily "sell well." This is a pitfall many optimizers encounter: spending significant budget testing a batch of creatives with CTR above 2% ("viral content"), only to find CVR below 1% and CPA through the roof.</p>
<p>The essential difference between running and selling lies in: <strong>Running solves the problem of "will users click?" while selling solves the problem of "will users buy?"</strong> Click behavior occurs at the ad level, conversion behavior occurs on landing pages and purchase flows. The target audiences for these two stages may not be the same people at all.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three Dimensions to Distinguish Running from Selling</h2>
<h3>1. Different Data Metrics</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Running Performance Standards</th>
<th>Selling Performance Standards</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CTR &gt; 1.5% (in-feed scenario)</td>
<td>CVR (add-to-cart rate) &gt; 3%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPM 20% below industry average</td>
<td>CPA within 1/3 of user LTV</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Relevance Score ≥ 8</td>
<td>ROAS ≥ 3 (e-commerce) / CPI &lt; $1.5 (App)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team once tested a batch of creatives with CTR as high as 3.2%, but add-to-cart rate was only 0.8%, with final CPA exceeding $45. Another batch with CTR of only 1.2% achieved 4.5% add-to-cart rate and controlled CPA within $18 because they accurately reached the target audience. Review found that high-CTR creatives attracted "curious users," while low-CTR creatives attracted "users with purchase intent."</p>
<h3>2. Different Lifecycle Patterns</h3>
<p>High-performing creatives often rise quickly and decay quickly. Typically, CTR begins declining after 3-5 days because Meta's algorithm, to maintain high click rates, gradually pushes creatives to broader audiences, reducing precision.</p>
<p>High-converting creatives rise slowly but decay slowly. Data from a utility app shows: high-CTR creatives average 7-day lifecycle, while high-CVR creatives can run stably for 14-21 days. Comprehensive calculation shows high-CVR creatives generate 2-3x cumulative conversions compared to high-CTR creatives.</p>
<h3>3. Different Audience Matching</h3>
<p>High-performing creatives attract "users interested in ads," while high-selling creatives attract "users with product needs." These sometimes overlap, sometimes completely diverge.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Practical Review: Two-Phase Testing Method</h2>
<h3>Phase 1: Creative Running Test (3-5 days)</h3>
<p>Small budget ($20-50 daily per creative) for quick validation:<br />
- <strong>CTR &lt; 1%</strong>: Creative lacks appeal, eliminate<br />
- <strong>CPM &gt; 150% of industry average</strong>: Targeting may be too narrow or creative competition too high<br />
- <strong>CTR &gt; 1.5% but abnormally low CPM</strong>: Beware of bonus hunters or non-target users</p>
<h3>Phase 2: Creative Selling Validation (7-14 days)</h3>
<p>Increase budget for Phase 1-passing creatives ($100+ daily per creative):<br />
- <strong>Monitor add-to-cart rate or registration completion rate</strong>: This is the first step where users express purchase intent<br />
- <strong>Calculate CPA and ROAS</strong>: Even with slightly higher CPM, it's a good creative if ROI is achievable<br />
- <strong>Track 7-day/14-day postback data</strong>: Some conversions are delayed; don't rely solely on daily data</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three Questions to Ask During Review</h2>
<p><strong>Question 1: High CTR but low CVR—who is clicking?</strong><br />
This typically means the ad creative attracted non-target users. It could be misaligned creative colors and copywriting style with target audience preferences, or hooks (like "free" or "limited time") attracting price-sensitive users rather than genuine target customers. Solution: resurvey users and optimize creative direction.</p>
<p><strong>Question 2: High CPM but low CPA—should we keep running?</strong><br />
This is often the result of precision targeting. If CPA is within acceptable range, consider scaling up, because precision audience competition will gradually decrease over time, potentially lowering CPM. A home goods brand once faced CPM as high as $45, but because the target audience was extremely precise, CPA was controlled within $25 with ROAS reaching 4.5.</p>
<p><strong>Question 3: Creative performance drops after two weeks—should we replace it?</strong><br />
Don't rush to replace. Check two data points: Is overall account CPA rising? If only single creative performance is declining, it may be due to excessive frequency for that creative within the account. If overall account CPA is rising, it means platform traffic quality is declining or competitor bids are increasing—in this case, replacing creatives won't help.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Summary</h2>
<p>The core standard for judging creative value isn't "how well it runs" but "how well it sells." CTR is the starting point, ROAS is the endpoint. During review, establish a complete data funnel of "CTR→CVR→CPA→ROAS," tracing every step. Only creatives that convert traffic into sales are truly valuable.</p>
